{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000155","statement":"Adenylosuccinase deficiency is caused by mutations in the ADSL gene and is inherited in an autosomal recessive fashion.","source_span":"Adenylosuccinase deficiency is caused by mutations in the ADSL gene and is inherited in an autosomal recessive fashion."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000155","statement":"Adenylosuccinase deficiency results from a lack of the enzyme adenylosuccinate lyase.","source_span":"results from a lack of the enzyme adenylosuccinate lyase"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000155","statement":"Seizures are observed in 60 percent of affected individuals and may begin within the first month of life.","source_span":"Seizures are observed in 60 percent of affected individuals."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000155","statement":"Growth delay has been observed in 30 percent of affected individuals in the first years of life, mainly related to feeding problems.","source_span":"growth delay has been observed in 30 percent of affected individuals, mainly related to feeding problems"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000155","statement":"Autism has been found to be present in one-third of cases of adenylosuccinase deficiency.","source_span":"Autism has been found to be present in one-third of cases."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000155","statement":"There are currently no effective therapies for adenylosuccinase deficiency; treatment is supportive based on specific features.","source_span":"At the current time, there are no effective therapies for the treatment of adenylosuccinase deficiency. Treatment is supportive based on the specific features."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000155","statement":"Almost all affected individuals experience delayed motor milestones ranging from mild to severe.","source_span":"Almost all affected individuals experience delayed motor milestones ranging from mild to severe."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000155","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What gene is associated with adenylosuccinase deficiency and how is it inherited?","answer":"Adenylosuccinase deficiency is caused by mutations in the ADSL gene and is inherited in an autosomal recessive fashion."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000155","fact_refs":[1],"question":"What enzyme is deficient in people with adenylosuccinase deficiency?","answer":"Adenylosuccinase deficiency results from a lack of the enzyme adenylosuccinate lyase."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000155","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What percentage of people with adenylosuccinase deficiency experience seizures, and when do they often begin?","answer":"Seizures occur in about 60 percent of affected individuals and may begin within the first month of life, often as the first sign of the condition."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000155","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What percentage of individuals with adenylosuccinase deficiency show growth delay in early life, and what is it mainly related to?","answer":"Growth delay has been observed in 30 percent of affected individuals in the first years of life, mainly related to feeding problems."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000155","fact_refs":[4],"question":"In what proportion of adenylosuccinase deficiency cases is autism present?","answer":"Autism has been found to be present in one-third of cases of adenylosuccinase deficiency."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000155","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What treatment options exist for adenylosuccinase deficiency?","answer":"There are currently no effective therapies for adenylosuccinase deficiency; treatment is supportive and based on the specific features present in each patient."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000158","statement":"Adiposis dolorosa is characterized by the growth of multiple, painful lipomas (benign, fatty tumors).","source_span":"growth of multiple, painful, lipomas (benign, fatty tumors)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000158","statement":"Adiposis dolorosa usually occurs in obese, post-menopausal women, but it can also occur in men.","source_span":"It usually occurs in obese, post-menopausal women, but it can also occur in men."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000158","statement":"Most cases of adiposis dolorosa are sporadic (not inherited), but a few familial cases with autosomal dominant inheritance have been reported.","source_span":"Most cases are sporadic (not inherited) but a few familial cases with autosomal dominant inheritance have been reported."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000158","statement":"Arthralgia (joint pain) occurs in approximately 90% of adiposis dolorosa patients.","source_span":"Arthralgia 90%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000158","statement":"Obesity occurs in approximately 90% of adiposis dolorosa patients.","source_span":"Obesity 90%"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000158","statement":"In familial autosomal dominant cases of adiposis dolorosa, each child of an affected person has a 50% (1 in 2) risk of inheriting the gene causing the condition.","source_span":"each child has a 50% (1 in 2) risk to inherit the gene causing the condition"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000158","statement":"Clinical genetic testing for adiposis dolorosa is currently not available because no specific associated gene has been identified.","source_span":"Clinical genetic testing for adiposis dolorosa is currently not available."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000158","statement":"Treatments for adiposis dolorosa pain may include prednisone or intravenous lidocaine, and no drug is known to change the course of the disease.","source_span":"prednisone or intravenous lidocaine for pain traditional pain medicines ... There is currently no drug known to change the course of the disease."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000158","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What condition is characterized by multiple painful lipomas and most commonly affects obese, post-menopausal women?","answer":"Adiposis dolorosa, a rare condition marked by multiple painful benign fatty tumors (lipomas), most commonly occurs in obese, post-menopausal women, though it can also occur in men."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000158","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What percentage of adiposis dolorosa patients experience arthralgia (joint pain)?","answer":"Arthralgia occurs in about 90% of patients with adiposis dolorosa."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000158","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What is known about the inheritance pattern of adiposis dolorosa?","answer":"Most cases of adiposis dolorosa are sporadic (not inherited); a few rare familial cases have shown autosomal dominant inheritance, but no associated genes have been identified."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000158","fact_refs":[4],"question":"If a parent has the rare familial autosomal dominant form of adiposis dolorosa, what is the risk to each of their children?","answer":"Each child has a 50% (1 in 2) risk of inheriting the gene causing the condition."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000158","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Why is clinical genetic testing not available for adiposis dolorosa?","answer":"Clinical genetic testing is not available because no specific gene associated with the condition has been identified, and most cases are sporadic."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000158","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What medications are used to manage pain from adiposis dolorosa lipomas?","answer":"Pain management may include prednisone or intravenous lidocaine, along with traditional pain medicines, though no drug is known to change the course of the disease."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000161","statement":"Adolescent idiopathic scoliosis is an abnormal curvature of the spine that appears in late childhood or adolescence, usually in an elongated 's' or 'C' shape with twisted/rotated spinal bones.","source_span":"an abnormal curvature of the spine that appears in late childhood or adolescence"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000161","statement":"Severe and progressive spinal curves occur more frequently in girls than in boys, for unknown reasons.","source_span":"severe and progressive curves occur more frequently in girls than in boys"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000161","statement":"The cause of adolescent idiopathic scoliosis is unknown, but it likely involves both genetic and environmental factors.","source_span":"The cause of adolescent idiopathic scoliosis is unknown. It is likely that there are both genetic and environmental factors involved."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000161","statement":"Treatment of adolescent idiopathic scoliosis may include observation, bracing, and/or surgery.","source_span":"Treatment may include observation, bracing and/or surgery."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000161","statement":"Common signs of adolescent idiopathic scoliosis include a tilt or unevenness in the shoulders, hips, or waist, or one leg appearing longer than the other.","source_span":"the most common signs of the condition include a tilt or unevenness (asymmetry) in the shoulders, hips, or waist, or having one leg that appears longer than the other"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000161","statement":"Having a close relative, such as a parent or sibling, with adolescent idiopathic scoliosis increases a child's risk of developing it.","source_span":"having a close relative (such as a parent or sibling) with the condition increases a child's risk of developing it"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d4_0000161","statement":"Scoliosis curves progress most during the patient's rapid growth period, i.e., the adolescent or pre-adolescent growth spurt.","source_span":"Curves progress most during the rapid growth period of the patient (adolescent or pre-adolescent growth spurt)."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000161","fact_refs":[0],"question":"What is adolescent idiopathic scoliosis?","answer":"It is an abnormal side-to-side curvature of the spine, usually in an elongated \"S\" or \"C\" shape with twisted or rotated spinal bones, that appears in late childhood or adolescence."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000161","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Are severe, progressive scoliosis curves more common in boys or girls?","answer":"Severe and progressive spinal curves occur more frequently in girls than in boys, for reasons that are not known."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000161","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What factors are believed to cause adolescent idiopathic scoliosis?","answer":"The exact cause is unknown (hence \"idiopathic\"), but it likely results from a combination of genetic and environmental factors, possibly involving hormonal, bone/muscle growth, or nervous system abnormalities."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000161","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What treatment options exist for adolescent idiopathic scoliosis?","answer":"Treatment may include observation, bracing, and/or surgery, with recommendations generally depending on the risk of curve progression."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000161","fact_refs":[5],"question":"When do scoliosis curves progress the most in adolescents?","answer":"Curves progress most during the patient's rapid growth period, i.e., the adolescent or pre-adolescent growth spurt."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d4_0000161","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are common physical signs that may indicate adolescent idiopathic scoliosis?","answer":"Common signs include a tilt or unevenness (asymmetry) in the shoulders, hips, or waist, or having one leg that appears longer than the other."}
